<p class="heritage-longread__dek">A standalone family history of the Rycroft line &mdash; Melanie&rsquo;s mother&rsquo;s father&rsquo;s people &mdash; from industrial Leeds, through the American Civil War and the coffee lands of Hawai&rsquo;i, to a homestead on the Peace River that gave its name to a town. It carries two other families with it: the <strong>Thommessens</strong> of Norway, who reach it through a sea-captain&rsquo;s orphaned daughter, and the <strong>Steinkes</strong> of the Sexsmith prairie. See the Notes at the end for what stands on record, what stands on the family tree, and what is still being chased.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-the-reach"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Prologue</span>The reach</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">Leeds → Hawai&rsquo;i → the Peace Country · 1843 to today</p><p>Most families in this larger story were <em>pushed</em> &mdash; off a cleared Hebridean croft, out of a famished Donegal townland, away from a Dutch polder drowned in cholera. The Rycrofts are the one family that seems, instead, to have been <em>flung</em>. Every generation lands farther from the last than any reasonable person would have gone: a back-street in industrial Leeds, then the saddle of a U.S. cavalry regiment, then the coffee slopes of Hawai&rsquo;i, then a quarter-section of bush on the Peace River where the snow lay six months a year. Nobody made them keep going. They simply kept reaching.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>They simply kept reaching.</p></aside><p>There is no single inherited word for it, the way the Dutch have <em>doorkomen</em> or the Hebrideans <em>buanseasmhachd</em>. The closest word is the plainest one: <strong>reach</strong>. Each Rycroft threw the line a little farther out into the world than it had any business going, and then trusted the next one to catch it. A sixteen-year-old left Leeds in 1859 and never came back. His son was born under a Hawaiian sky and died under an Alberta one. His granddaughter&rsquo;s daughter is raising three children in Grande Prairie who have a Honolulu street and an Alberta town both named after the same family &mdash; a coincidence no other family in this book can match.</p><p>This is also the most <em>adventurous</em> of the family lines, and the hardest to pin to paper, because adventurers leave gaps. The Hawaiian and Alberta chapters are richly documented &mdash; newspapers, obituaries, a town&rsquo;s own naming story, the family&rsquo;s own tree. The deepest Leeds origins are thinner, and an earlier attempt to fill them produced a wholly invented English ancestry that this account has thrown out (see the Notes). So the story is told, like the others, in two voices: warm where the record is warm, and careful &mdash; <em>probably</em>, <em>the tree says</em>, <em>not yet confirmed</em> &mdash; where it is thin.</p><p>This is the story of how far one family reached, told one crossing at a time.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-the-leeds-the-family-left-james-robert-rycroft-e"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter One</span>The Leeds the family left &mdash; <em>James Robert Rycroft, early 1800s</em></h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">early 1800s · industrial Leeds, Yorkshire</p><p>The Rycroft line begins, as far as the family&rsquo;s own tree can see it, in <strong>Leeds</strong> in the first decades of the nineteenth century, with a couple recorded as <strong>James Robert Rycroft</strong> and <strong>Eliza Brodgen</strong> (the clerks also wrote it <em>Brogden</em>). They are the bottom of the documented tree &mdash; and an honest one must say at once that they sit there on the family&rsquo;s inherited record, not yet on a primary one. The tree gives them a son, <strong>Samuel</strong>, born in Leeds in 1840, and another, <strong>Robert Henry</strong>, born 27 April 1843, with the household placed at <strong>Whitkirk</strong>, a township on the eastern edge of Leeds. Those facts are consistent and plausible; they are not yet nailed to a census page or a parish register. Treat them as the family&rsquo;s best memory of its own beginning, awaiting the proof (see the Notes &mdash; and note that a different, fabricated &ldquo;Essex&rdquo; ancestry has been discarded).</p><p>What is not in doubt is the <em>kind</em> of place they lived in. Early-Victorian Leeds was one of the engines of the world &mdash; a West Riding city of flax mills and woollen finishing-shops, of foundries and dye-works and back-to-back houses crammed along the Aire, the sky brown with mill-smoke, the river running with the waste of the trades. A Rycroft family on the Whitkirk edge of it lived where the city met the last of the fields, in the churn of people the factories pulled in from the countryside.</p><p>There is a coincidence buried here that no one alive at the time could have seen. A few miles west, in the woollen villages of the Aire valley around Calverley and Idle, another working Yorkshire family was raising children in exactly these decades &mdash; the <strong>Haistes</strong>, clothiers and weavers, whose own line would one day cross an ocean to the very same corner of Alberta. Two Yorkshire textile-belt families, a long day&rsquo;s walk apart, who never met and never knew the other existed; whose descendants would marry, a hundred and sixty years later, in the Peace Country of Canada. The Rycroft river and the Haiste river both rise in the same Yorkshire rain. They would not join for six generations.</p><p>And then, around <strong>1859</strong>, the Rycroft line did the thing it would do in every generation afterward. A son &mdash; Robert Henry, sixteen years old &mdash; left Leeds, crossed the Atlantic, and never returned. The family that had been Yorkshire for as long as anyone could remember began, with that one boy&rsquo;s passage, to come apart from England for good.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-robert-henry-rycroft-sr-1843-1909-the-reach-acro"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Two</span>Robert Henry Rycroft Sr. (1843&ndash;1909) &mdash; the reach across the world</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1843&ndash;1909 · Leeds → the U.S. Cavalry → Honolulu &amp; Puna</p><p>If the family has a founding adventurer, it is this man &mdash; and his life is so improbable that it would read as invention if a Honolulu newspaper had not printed much of it while he was alive.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--right"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/img/heritage/rycroft/rycroft-ch02-civil-war-cavalry.jpg' ); ?>" alt="An American Civil War cavalry camp with tents, soldiers, and horses" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>An American Civil War cavalry camp &mdash; the kind of outfit a Leeds emigrant boy rode with for sixteen months. <em>(18th Pennsylvania Cavalry; U.S. Library of Congress, public domain.)</em></figcaption></figure><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>The man could not stand still.</p></aside><p><strong>Robert Henry Rycroft</strong> was born in Leeds on <strong>27 April 1843</strong>. At sixteen he emigrated to the United States. And there, into the bargain, he did something almost no English emigrant boy did: he <strong>joined the cavalry and rode in the American Civil War</strong> &mdash; about sixteen months in the saddle, by the account preserved in his obituary and the Hawaiian histories. (Which regiment, and on which side, no muster roll has yet been found to say; it is one of the open chases in the Notes.) A Leeds teenager who had been in America only a year or two found himself in the largest war the continent had ever seen.</p><p>When the war let him go, he did not go home to Yorkshire. He went <em>farther</em> &mdash; to the <strong>Kingdom of Hawai&rsquo;i</strong>, then an independent island monarchy in the middle of the Pacific, and he arrived among the small, busy community of English-speaking tradesmen building its modern economy. He worked first at the <strong>Honolulu Iron Works</strong>, then made plumbing his trade; he ran the Fountain restaurant and a temperance saloon on Fort Street; he even spent an interlude in <strong>Brisbane, Australia</strong>, where he is credited with erecting the colony&rsquo;s first ammonia ice machine. The man could not stand still.</p><p>In <strong>1872</strong> he married &mdash; and the marriage tied the Rycrofts into the colonial establishment of the islands. His wife was <strong>Elizabeth P. Campbell</strong>, the <strong>sister of A.N. Campbell, Treasurer of Hawai&rsquo;i</strong> (later an officer of the Henry Waterhouse Trust Company in Honolulu). Their first child, a son they named Henry, was born that same year. <em>(This Campbell is a Hawai&rsquo;i-Scottish family of the territorial business class &mdash; no relation to the Hebridean Campbells of South Uist who reach these same children from Thomas&rsquo;s side. Two unrelated Campbells, one Pacific and one Atlantic, in one family tree.)</em></p><p>Then he reached again &mdash; this time to the <strong>Big Island</strong>. From about <strong>1877 to 1899</strong> Robert Henry Rycroft was a pioneer of the <strong>Puna</strong> district: he bought the <strong>Pohoiki and Keahialeka tracts</strong>, some nine thousand acres of lava-country, ran cattle and an <em>&lsquo;awa</em> trade, built a <strong>sawmill at Pohoiki</strong> that supplied hardwood to Honolulu by schooner, and &mdash; most lasting of all &mdash; became <strong>one of the first men in Hawai&rsquo;i to cultivate coffee on a large scale</strong>, building a coffee mill in 1891. He was, in the plainest sense, a frontiersman in the tropics: clearing, planting, milling, shipping, on ground that had been forest and lava a generation before.</p><p>In <strong>1899</strong> he came back to Honolulu and started the <strong>Fountain Soda Works</strong>, on the old baseball-ground property in what is now the Sheridan tract &mdash; and the city remembers him still, because the street that runs through that ground is <strong>Rycroft Street, Honolulu</strong>, named for the family and its soda works. He died in Honolulu on <strong>3 February 1909</strong>, called in his obituary one of the oldest residents of the Hawaiian Islands. He and Elizabeth had raised at least five children whose names survive &mdash; sons <strong>Henry, Mark,</strong> and <strong>Walter</strong>, daughters <strong>Sophia</strong> and <strong>Gladys</strong> &mdash; a Hawaiian-born generation who would scatter as far as their father had reached: Mark Alexander would die in <strong>Edmonton</strong>, Gladys in <strong>California</strong>, and Henry, the eldest, would carry the line to the Peace River.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-robert-henry-rycroft-1872-1944-the-islander-who-"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Three</span>Robert Henry Rycroft (1872&ndash;1944) &mdash; the islander who chose the frontier</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1872&ndash;1944 · Honolulu → the town of Rycroft, Alberta</p><p>The eldest son carried his father&rsquo;s whole name and, it turned out, his father&rsquo;s restlessness. <strong>Robert Henry Rycroft</strong> was born in <strong>Honolulu on 9 May 1872</strong>, the year his parents married &mdash; a child of the soda works and the coffee lands, who grew up among the ships of many nations in a harbour town in the last decades of the Hawaiian Kingdom. He took up the family trade; for some years he ran the soda business his father had built, and later styled himself an importer and commission agent, moving goods across the Pacific.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>The slip said Rycroft.</p></aside><p>He might have stayed an islander all his life. Instead, in his thirties, he reached one more time &mdash; and chose, of all the places on earth, the cold opposite of everything he had known.</p><p>In <strong>1906</strong> he met a young Norwegian woman, <strong>Helene Thommessen</strong>, who was in Honolulu (she has a chapter of her own; it is one of the strangest threads in this whole book). They were together by <strong>1909</strong>, when their first son, Eric, was born; they married in Honolulu on <strong>29 June 1911</strong>. <em>(The son&rsquo;s birth two years before the recorded marriage is a real wrinkle the records have not yet smoothed &mdash; see the Notes; the family simply held both dates and moved on.)</em> For their honeymoon they travelled up through <strong>British Columbia</strong> &mdash; and there, far from the tropics, they heard about the <strong>Peace River Country</strong> of northern Alberta, where the Dominion was throwing open free homestead land at the very end of the continent&rsquo;s frontier.</p><p>A man born under palm trees did not have to want a quarter-section of frozen bush eight thousand kilometres north. Robert Henry Rycroft wanted it. By <strong>1912</strong> he and Helene had registered two parcels near <strong>Spirit River</strong> &mdash; SW 25-78-6-W6 in his name, the east half of 26 in hers &mdash; and the family left Hawai&rsquo;i for good, trading the harbour of Honolulu for a sod-and-log frontier where the nearest railhead was days away.</p><p>They threw themselves into building the place. The very <strong>first meeting of the Spirit River Rural Municipality</strong> was held in the Rycroft home on <strong>2 January 1917</strong>; Robert Henry served as its secretary-treasurer, sat on the school board, and was a Justice of the Peace for many years. And in <strong>1920</strong>, when the district that had split off from Spirit River needed a name, four pioneers &mdash; <strong>R.H. Rycroft, W.S.O. &ldquo;Billy&rdquo; English, H.E. &ldquo;Doc&rdquo; Calkin,</strong> and <strong>George Garnett</strong> &mdash; wrote their names on slips of paper, dropped them in a hat, and drew one out.</p><p>The slip said <strong>Rycroft</strong>.</p><p>So the family that had a street in Honolulu now had a town on the Peace &mdash; the town of <strong>Rycroft, Alberta</strong>, about 25 km northwest of Sexsmith, still on the map today. <em>(For some fourteen years the post office misspelled itself &ldquo;Roycroft,&rdquo; until the Board of Trade got it corrected in 1933 to match the railway&rsquo;s spelling. And local history is firm that the town honours this R.H. Rycroft, the frontier Justice of the Peace &mdash; not, as one outside guess has it, an unconnected English baronet; see the Notes.)</em> Robert Henry Rycroft died at <strong>Sexsmith on 30 March 1944</strong>, aged seventy-one, and was buried at <strong>Teepee Creek</strong>. The islander had become, in the end, the founding settler of a prairie town.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-helene-thommessen-1885-1958-the-sea-captain-s-or"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Four</span>Helene Thommessen (1885&ndash;1958) &mdash; the sea-captain&rsquo;s orphan</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1885&ndash;1958 · Larvik, Norway → Honolulu → the Peace</p><p>The woman who crossed the Pacific to marry into the Rycrofts carried the most heartbreaking reach of all, and she carried it alone almost from birth.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--right"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/img/heritage/rycroft/rycroft-ch04-barque.jpg' ); ?>" alt="A three-masted barque under sail in the 1870s" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A three-masted barque of the 1870s &mdash; the kind of ship Captain Theodor Thommessen, Helene&rsquo;s father, mastered until yellow fever took him in Brazil. <em>(Ship portrait, 1872; Wikimedia Commons, public domain.)</em></figcaption></figure><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>By the age of four, Helene was an orphan.</p></aside><p><strong>Helene Lovise Christiane Thommessen</strong> was born on <strong>28 February 1885</strong> in <strong>Larvik</strong>, a sailing, whaling, and timber port on the Vestfold coast of southern Norway. Her father was a <strong>ship&rsquo;s captain &mdash; Theodor Thommessen Holtan</strong>, born at Borre in 1854, master of the barque <em>Vanadis</em>. Her mother was <strong>Helene Lovise Juel</strong>, of a family rooted around Nykirke and the Drammen fjord. The child was named for her mother &mdash; and lost her almost at once: <strong>Helene Lovise Juel died in 1885, the same year her daughter was born.</strong> Four years later, in <strong>1889</strong>, Captain Thommessen died of <strong>yellow fever in the port of Santos, Brazil</strong>, half a world from home, on the kind of voyage that had kept him away her whole short life with him. By the age of four, Helene was an <strong>orphan</strong> &mdash; a sea-captain&rsquo;s daughter with a dead mother, a father buried in South America, and a brother, Thomas, two years older than she was.</p><p>How a Norwegian orphan girl from Larvik came to be standing in Honolulu by 1906 is the single thing about her the records have not yet given up. The likeliest road ran through the great Norwegian sea-and-emigrant networks &mdash; out across the Atlantic, on west to the Scandinavian communities of the American Pacific Northwest, and from there by ship to the islands &mdash; but the passenger lists that would prove it have not been found. What is certain is the arrival: somewhere in that harbour town the captain&rsquo;s orphaned daughter met the soda-maker&rsquo;s restless son, and the two of them, each carrying an ocean behind them, decided to reach once more &mdash; together, north, to the Peace.</p><p>She would live a long Alberta life after all the leaving. Helene Rycroft died at <strong>Grande Prairie on 28 July 1958</strong> and was buried beside her husband at <strong>Teepee Creek</strong>, three-quarters of a century and a full circumnavigation away from the Larvik quay where she began. Through her, a strain of Norwegian seafaring runs in three children in Grande Prairie who have never seen the North Sea.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-eric-jarman-rycroft-1909-1993-the-hawaiian-born-"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Five</span>Eric Jarman Rycroft (1909&ndash;1993) &mdash; the Hawaiian-born homesteader</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1909&ndash;1993 · born in Hawai&rsquo;i, a farmer at Teepee Creek</p><p>The first child of Robert Henry and Helene was <strong>Eric Jarman Rycroft</strong>, born in <strong>Hawai&rsquo;i on 30 January 1909</strong> &mdash; which made him, all his long Alberta life, the man with the magical fact at the family table: <em>born in Honolulu.</em> He had no memory of it. He was a small child when the family sailed for British Columbia and went north to the Peace in 1912, so the boy who had opened his eyes under a tropical sky grew up instead among the snowdrifts and stooks of the Teepee Creek district, in a frontier that could not have been less like the one his parents left.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--left"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/img/heritage/rycroft/rycroft-ch05-prairie-homestead.jpg' ); ?>" alt="A homestead and team of horses on the Saskatchewan prairie, early 1900s" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A prairie homestead of the homestead era &mdash; the frozen frontier the Hawaiian-born boy grew up on. <em>(J. Powell&rsquo;s farm, Kenaston, Saskatchewan; Library and Archives Canada, public domain.)</em></figcaption></figure><p>He became a Peace Country farmer, plainly and for good. On <strong>26 November 1933</strong>, at <strong>Sexsmith</strong>, he married <strong>Laureta Maude Janette Clark</strong> &mdash; herself a daughter of the new west, born across the line at <strong>Centralia, Washington</strong>, in 1911. Together, on the land around <strong>Teepee Creek</strong>, they raised a large prairie family: among them <strong>Geneva Maude</strong> (1934), <strong>Samuel Eric John</strong> &mdash; &ldquo;Sam,&rdquo; the one through whom this line comes down (1935) &mdash; <strong>Dennison</strong> (1937), and more. Eric lived through both world wars, the coming of the tractor and the highway and the telephone to the Peace, and the slow growth of Grande Prairie from a railway settlement into a city. He died at <strong>Teepee Creek on 4 January 1993</strong>, aged eighty-three, the last of the family who could honestly say he had been born in the Kingdom of Hawai&rsquo;i&rsquo;s old territory and had broken Alberta snow with his own boots.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-sam-rycroft-1935-2015-bette-steinke-1942-2025-th"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Six</span>Sam Rycroft (1935&ndash;2015) &amp; Bette Steinke (1942&ndash;2025) &mdash; the Sexsmith generation</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1935&ndash;2015 · Sexsmith · Sam Rycroft &amp; Bette Steinke</p><p><strong>Samuel Eric John &ldquo;Sam&rdquo; Rycroft</strong> was born at <strong>Sexsmith on 15 July 1935</strong>, in the depths of the Depression, into a world of horses, wood stoves, and neighbours who got each other&rsquo;s crops in. He grew up on the Teepee Creek land, came of age as the Peace Country electrified and mechanized around him, and stayed &mdash; a working Alberta man of his generation, near the ground his grandfather had named a town beside.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>They share a stone in the Peace Country ground.</p></aside><p>On <strong>30 October 1959</strong>, at Sexsmith, Sam married <strong>Bette Doreen Steinke</strong> &mdash; and with her a new family river joins the Rycroft one.</p><p><strong>The Steinkes.</strong> Bette was born at <strong>Sexsmith on 26 April 1942</strong>, the <strong>fourth child of Henry and Martha Steinke</strong> &mdash; a German family of the Sexsmith district, almost certainly <strong>Lutheran</strong> (a cluster of Steinkes lies in the Emmaus Lutheran Cemetery at Teepee Creek, the German Protestant ground of that country). The Steinkes belong to the great wave of German-speaking settlers who came to the Canadian prairies between the 1890s and the 1920s &mdash; many of them by way of the German colonies of the Russian Empire or the eastern reaches of Prussia and Pomerania, often homesteading first in southern Saskatchewan before pushing north to the Peace. Exactly where Henry and Martha&rsquo;s people came from, and by which road, is not yet traced past them &mdash; it is the next Steinke record to chase (see the Notes). But the shape is the prairie&rsquo;s most common one: a German Lutheran farming family, holding on through hard winters in a new country, whose daughter married the grandson of a man born in Honolulu. Bette lived until <strong>19 January 2025</strong>, dying at Grande Prairie at eighty-two &mdash; recent enough that she is not a figure from the archives but a grandmother remembered.</p><p>Sam and Bette raised four children in the Grande Prairie country &mdash; <strong>Lorne</strong> (1960), <strong>Lana</strong> (1961), <strong>Vance</strong> (1963), and <strong>Clark</strong> (1965) &mdash; a busy, hard-working home near where five generations of the family had now lived. Sam died at <strong>Badheart, Alberta, on 23 July 2015</strong>; Bette, ten years later. They share a stone in the Peace Country ground.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-where-the-rycroft-river-joins-the-others"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">The Living Line</span>Where the Rycroft river joins the others</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">Grande Prairie · the confluence of five families</p><p>Every long family story has a moment when it stops being history and becomes the present. For the Rycrofts, that moment is now.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>The line that always reached outward reached, this last time, back &mdash; to keep a name.</p></aside><p>The line comes down through Sam and Bette&rsquo;s daughter, <strong>Lana Lyn Rycroft</strong>, born at <strong>Grande Prairie on 29 September 1961</strong>. Lana married <strong>Dan Steven Haiste</strong> &mdash; of the Yorkshire-and-prairie Haistes, another family that had reached the Peace Country from far away &mdash; and they raised two daughters, <strong>Misty</strong> and <strong>Melanie</strong>. Lana worked hard and loved her girls through a fast-changing northern Alberta, and she died too soon, at <strong>Edmonton on 7 May 2015</strong> &mdash; the same hard year that took her father Sam. She is the bridge between the old Rycroft story and the living one, and the family carries her gently.</p><p>Her daughter is <strong>Melanie Lyn Haiste</strong>, born at Grande Prairie on <strong>25 May 1983</strong> &mdash; and in Melanie two whole families meet. Through her father Dan she carries the <strong>Haiste</strong> line out of the Yorkshire tanneries and the Saskatchewan dust; through her mother Lana she carries the <strong>Rycroft</strong> line &mdash; Leeds, Hawai&rsquo;i, the Peace &mdash; and, beside it, the <strong>Steinke</strong> line of the Sexsmith prairie and the <strong>Thommessen</strong> line of the Norwegian sea. Four families reaching, from four directions, into one woman.</p><p>Melanie and <strong>Thomas Cheesman</strong> &mdash; who married in Calgary on <strong>2 July 2016</strong> and are the co-parents of three children &mdash; pass everything they carry, jointly, to those three, raised in Grande Prairie:</p><ul>
<li><strong>Patience Kristie-Anne Cheesman</strong>, born 24 September 2013</li>
<li><strong>Daniel Eric Thomas Cheesman</strong>, born 30 June 2015</li>
<li><strong>Faith Lana Lyn Cheesman</strong>, born 30 March 2017</li>
</ul><p>They inherit it together &mdash; not one heir, but a sibling-group, each carrying the whole. And the Rycroft current does not arrive alone. It is one of several now running in a single household: the Lakeman and the Docherty and the Hebridean McIver lines through their father Thomas, the Haiste and the Rycroft lines through their mother Melanie, and the Cheesman name &mdash; chosen, not inherited &mdash; that gathers them all.</p><p>None of the Rycrofts who reached so far could have pictured these three. The Leeds boy who sailed at sixteen could not have imagined Hawai&rsquo;i; the soda-maker&rsquo;s son could not have imagined the Peace; Helene, orphaned in Larvik, could not have imagined dying old under an Alberta sky among grandchildren who would never see the sea. But there is one quiet thing they would recognise, if they knew it. The youngest of the three children is named <strong>Faith Lana Lyn</strong> &mdash; and <em>Lana Lyn</em> is her grandmother&rsquo;s name, carried forward into a child Lana did not live to meet. The line that always reached outward reached, this last time, <em>back</em> &mdash; to keep a name.</p><p>That is what <em>reach</em> was always for. Patience, Daniel, and Faith inherit it together. The world they will live through is not yet written. But the family has been here before &mdash; it has crossed every ocean there is &mdash; and it has always, somehow, reached the far shore.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-lineage-at-a-glance">Lineage at a glance</h2><div class="heritage-longread__table-wrap"><table class="heritage-longread__table">
