</table></div><p><strong>Confidence tiers:</strong> <em>Verified</em> &mdash; anchored to at least one primary or contemporary record (newspaper, obituary, civil registration, the family bible/tree where it agrees with a record). <em>Probable</em> &mdash; rests on the family tree, consistent but not yet primary-confirmed. <em>Living memory</em> &mdash; recent enough to be remembered directly by family.</p><div class="heritage-longread__ribbon" aria-hidden="true"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">James Robert</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">early 1800s</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">R.H. Rycroft Sr.</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1843&ndash;1909</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Robert Henry</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1872&ndash;1944</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Eric Jarman</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1909&ndash;1993</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Sam</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1935&ndash;2015</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Lana</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1961&ndash;2015</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Melanie</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">b. 1983</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Patience · Daniel · Faith</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">2013&ndash;2017</span></span></div><p class="heritage-longread__aside"><em>Side-branches named in the records but off the direct line: Robert Henry Rycroft Sr.&rsquo;s other Hawai&rsquo;i-born children &mdash; <strong>Mark Alexander</strong> (1878 Honolulu &ndash; 1955 Edmonton), <strong>Walter</strong>, <strong>Sophia</strong>, and <strong>Gladys</strong> (c. 1890 &ndash; 1990, California); the 1872 son&rsquo;s other children with Helene &mdash; an infant <strong>Elizabeth</strong> (1912), <strong>Robert Henry</strong> (1913&ndash;2003), and <strong>Theodore Alexander</strong> (1915&ndash;1957), whose many descendants fill the Peace Country today; Eric and Laureta&rsquo;s other children alongside Sam &mdash; <strong>Geneva Maude</strong> (1934&ndash;2019), <strong>Dennison</strong> (1937), and their siblings; Sam and Bette&rsquo;s other children alongside Lana &mdash; <strong>Lorne, Vance,</strong> and <strong>Clark</strong>; and Melanie&rsquo;s sister <strong>Misty Dawn</strong>. R.H. Sr.&rsquo;s brother <strong>Samuel Rycroft</strong> (b. 1840, Leeds) stands at the head of the line. The three children at the foot of the table inherit the Rycroft line jointly with the Haiste, Lakeman, Docherty, and McIver lines, gathered under the chosen Cheesman name.</em></p><details class="heritage-longread__notes">
<summary>Notes on the records</summary>
<div class="heritage-longread__notes-body">
<p>The Rycroft line is, oddly, best-documented in its most exotic middle &mdash; the Hawaiian decades &mdash; and thinnest at its English root. That is the opposite of most families, and it is because the middle generation was newsworthy (a coffee pioneer, a soda-works founder, a town&rsquo;s namesake) while the Leeds beginning was ordinary and poor. The story is solid from Hawai&rsquo;i forward; the deep Yorkshire origin is the work that remains.</p><h3>Key records still needed</h3><p>In rough order of value:</p><ol>
<li><strong>The 1851 (and 1841) England census for Whitkirk / Leeds</strong>, and the <strong>Leeds baptisms</strong> of Robert Henry (1843) and Samuel (1840) &mdash; to turn the parents <strong>James Robert Rycroft &amp; Eliza Brodgen</strong> from a tree lead into a verified fact, and to push the Yorkshire line back further.</li>
<li><strong>Robert Henry Rycroft Sr.&rsquo;s U.S. Civil War record</strong> &mdash; the regiment, the side, the enlistment. Sixteen months in the cavalry ought to leave a muster roll or a pension file (try the variants Rycroft / Roycroft / Rycraft).</li>
<li><strong>Helene Thommessen&rsquo;s Larvik baptism (1885)</strong> in the Norwegian Digital Archives, and <strong>her emigration/route to Hawai&rsquo;i</strong> by 1906 &mdash; the one missing link in an otherwise vivid story.</li>
<li><strong>The Campbell family of Hawai&rsquo;i</strong> &mdash; A.N. Campbell (the Territory&rsquo;s Treasurer) and his sister Elizabeth&rsquo;s own origins; and the <strong>primary Honolulu marriage record</strong> (trees say 21 August 1871; the obituary says 1872).</li>
<li><strong>The Steinke origin</strong> &mdash; Henry and Martha Steinke&rsquo;s parents, birthplaces, and the European point of departure, via Bette&rsquo;s 1959 Sexsmith marriage record, the 1931 census, and a direct surname search of the Peace Country local histories (<em>Wagon Trails Grown Over: Sexsmith to the Smoky</em>; <em>Pioneers of the Peace</em>).</li>
</ol><h3>Notes</h3><p><strong>A discarded false ancestry.</strong> An earlier AI-generated family keepsake invented a deep English line &mdash; <em>&ldquo;Rycraft&rdquo; of Little Bentley, Essex,</em> running back to the early 1700s (a Thomas, a Jacob, a Robert &amp; Mary Smyth). <strong>None of it is in the family tree, and it has been discarded.</strong> It appears to be an unrelated real Essex family grafted on to fill a gap. The honest deep root of this family is Leeds, not Essex; everything before Robert Henry Rycroft Sr. (b. 1843 Leeds) is, for now, the probable Leeds couple and no further.</p><p><strong>The Hawaiian chapter is unusually well-sourced</strong> for a family history &mdash; through the <em>Hawaiian Star</em> obituary of 3 February 1909, the <em>Pacific Commercial Advertiser</em> (9 March 1900) on the family&rsquo;s return from Puna, the standard reference <em>Place Names of Hawaii</em> on Rycroft Street, and the &ldquo;Images of Old Hawaii&rdquo; historical synthesis. The Puna land, the coffee mill, the soda works, the Iron Works, the Campbell marriage, and the children&rsquo;s roster all rest on these contemporary sources.</p><p><strong>The two Robert Henrys.</strong> Family memory had compressed father and son into one figure &mdash; the &ldquo;Robert Henry Rycroft, born 1872, who ran a sugar and coffee plantation.&rdquo; The records separate them: the <strong>coffee, cattle, and sawmill empire of Puna belonged to the father (1843&ndash;1909)</strong>; the <strong>son (1872&ndash;1944)</strong> ran the Honolulu soda works and then became the Alberta settler and town namesake. Both are real; the enterprise was the father&rsquo;s, the homestead the son&rsquo;s.</p><p><strong>The town-name question &mdash; settled in the family&rsquo;s favour.</strong> Local histories by Eric J. Rycroft and George Potter, and the contemporary record, credit the town of Rycroft to <strong>R.H. Rycroft the settler</strong> (the hat-draw of 1920). An outside guess attributing it to a British baronet does not hold up &mdash; the Rycroft baronetcy (of Calton, Yorkshire) has no documented connection to the Peace Country, and the local sources are unanimous.</p><p><strong>The 1909-before-1911 gap.</strong> Eric&rsquo;s birth (30 January 1909) precedes his parents&rsquo; recorded marriage (29 June 1911) by more than two years. The records do not yet explain it &mdash; an earlier informal union, a delayed or re-entered registration, or simple period record-keeping are all possible. It is set down here as fact, not smoothed over.</p><p><strong>Where the line is solid.</strong> From the Hawaiian generation forward &mdash; Robert Henry (1872), Helene, Eric, Sam and Bette, Lana, and the living family &mdash; the story rests on the family&rsquo;s own well-kept tree, contemporary records, obituaries, and living memory. The work that remains is almost all on the far side of the ocean: in Leeds, in Larvik, and in the German parish the Steinkes came from.</p><p class="heritage-longread__aside"><em>This is a working draft, built on the family tree, the Hawaiian contemporary record, and a verified Steinke obituary, with the open chases named above. It will be revised as the Leeds, Larvik, and Steinke records give up more of what they hold. The aim is the family&rsquo;s: confident where the evidence is strong, honest where it is thin, and never willing to invent a parish or a parent to make a sentence read more smoothly than the truth allows.</em></p>
